  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Towards Resilient Seafood systems in Kenya, Tanzania and Indonesia, support to the Nature Conservancy. 

The Project&#x2019;s expected effect on society is to ensure that coastal communities in Indonesia, Kenya and Tanzania are active, skilled participants in designing climate-ready approaches to managing their coastal resources, increasing resilience and the stability of local livelihoods and seafood supplies, while restoring critical habitats and sustaining healthy fish stocks (Impact).

The expected effects for the target group of the Project are:
Outcome 1: At least 5 small-scale coastal fisheries are on the path to sustainability via the co-development of science-based management action plans that will identify short- and medium-term steps and capacity trainings needed to implement improved management measures. These action plans are with 4 communities and supported by CSOs (Civil Society Organizations) and government institutions in Kenya and Tanzania.
 
Outcome 2: Increased transparency in fish catch documentation through electronic monitoring, VMS (Vessel Monitoring System) and electronic logbooks in the artisanal and commercial tuna and snapper fisheries in Indonesia (2 fisheries) and industrial tuna fisheries in Kenya (1 fishery), Improved data collection and monitoring at-sea will contribute to increased science-based management and compliance with existing fisheries regulations.
 
Outcome 3: Restorative Aquaculture: Farmer yields increase, and environmental conditions are enhanced on at least 500 hectares of seaweed farms in Tanzania, Kenya, and Indonesia.</narrative>
  </description>
